OK, I see now.  I didn't realize the importance
sampling was for the integral --- I thought it
was for approximate draws from the posterior.
And now I get GMO:MML::ADVI:VI --- we're just
talking about p(phi | y) here.

What you do with the phi* you get out of GMO
is your business!

Which brings up the question of what you (or lme4)
do for alpha* if MML only gives you a
Laplace approximation to p(phi | y).

And is what people call "empirical Bayes" just
full Bayes for p(alpha | phi*, y)?

- Bob

P.S.  Dustin --- I'm putting this in Bayesian terms
and avoiding max likelihood and std errors on purpose,
because we're going to have a prior and use the usual
Bayesian manipulations to get the MML estimate as the
approximate posterior mean if the prior is uniform.

