I agree.  We'll need some more gentle introductions, even
for people with good math skills (I took topology and a year of
analysis as an undergrad math major, and I knew the basics
of measure theory coming in).

The best intro I've ever read to measure theory is in
the appendix to Anderson and Moore's book on Optimal Filtering.
It just lays out all the definitions very concisely and
consistently in a way very few other sources do.  But no examples,
so it's more of a good way to consolidate understanding than to
learn from.

- Bob

