I wasn't saying don't do calc, I was just saying that you
could do simpler examples that aren't crazily behaved
trig functions, such as normal -> lognormal, or gamma to
inverse gamma.

It's one of those things that if they aren't already fluent
with changes of variables, your multivariate example is a
difficult place to start.

But I already see we're going to need a more gentle intro to
get readers to the point where they can read what you're
writing, so you can probably get away sticking to abstractions.

>> It's all slightly unusual because rather than introducing
>> abstract sigma-algebras and random variables, everything's
>> baked into the space, so all the random variables are
>> projections (although the whole issue of random variables
>> is sidestepped).  This makes it easy to generate sample spaces
