> I wasn't saying don't do calc, I was just saying that you
> could do simpler examples that aren't crazily behaved
> trig functions, such as normal -> lognormal, or gamma to
> inverse gamma.
>
> It's one of those things that if they aren't already fluent
> with changes of variables, your multivariate example is a
> difficult place to start.

Yeah, but the others don’t lead to much difference visually
and they introduce the additional complication of an added
constraint.  I had to carefully construct this one to be
visually striking in the figure.

> In the setup you have, random variables are just projections.
> But if you define random variables, I haven't seen it yet.
> Nor do you really talk about the required projections, but
> rather do it implicitly by imposing structure on your sample
> space (specificially product structure with dimensions correspodning
> to [components of] random variables of interest).

But in the end random variables are just maps between
probability triples and they don’t actually end up being
all that useful concepts in practice, hence I don’t introduce
them explicitly at all.  I just don’t see the need to do such
and never have.

